var e={level:`B1`,type:`error-correct`,questions:[{id:`b1-ec-001`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After didn't use the base form of the verb: didn't go.`,wrongReasons:{"he didn't went to school yesterday":`After didn't the verb stays in the base form: go, not went.`},wrong:`He didn't went to school yesterday.`,solutions:[`He didn't go to school yesterday.`,`He did not go to school yesterday.`]},{id:`b1-ec-002`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect (simple or continuous) with 'since', not the present continuous.`,wrongReasons:{"i live here since 2010":`'since' + a starting point needs the present perfect (have lived), not the present simple.`,"i am living here since 2010":`'since' + a starting point needs the present perfect, not the present continuous.`},wrong:`I am living here since 2010.`,solutions:[`I have lived here since 2010.`,`I have been living here since 2010.`]},{id:`b1-ec-003`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The present perfect is have/has + past participle: She has written three books.`,wrongReasons:{"she has wrote three novels since 2015":`'Wrote' is the past simple. After 'has' you need the past participle 'written'.`,"she has writed three novels since 2015":`'Write' is irregular: write - wrote - written. 'Writed' does not exist.`},wrong:`She has wrote three novels since 2015.`,solutions:[`She has written three novels since 2015.`,`She's written three novels since 2015.`]},{id:`b1-ec-004`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'since' with a point in time and 'for' with a period: since 2012, for ten years.`,wrongReasons:{"i have known marco for 2012":`2012 is a point in time, so you need 'since', not 'for'.`,"i know marco since 2012":`With 'since' you need the present perfect (have known), not the present simple.`},wrong:`I have known Marco for 2012.`,solutions:[`I have known Marco since 2012.`,`I've known Marco since 2012.`]},{id:`b1-ec-005`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'for' with a period of time: We have lived here for six years.`,wrongReasons:{"we have lived in this flat since six years":`'Six years' is a period of time, so use 'for', not 'since'.`,"we have lived in this flat from six years":`'From' is not used here. For a period of time up to now, use 'for'.`},wrong:`We have lived in this flat since six years.`,solutions:[`We have lived in this flat for six years.`,`We have been living in this flat for six years.`,`We've lived in this flat for six years.`,`We've been living in this flat for six years.`]},{id:`b1-ec-006`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After has/have use the past participle: The bus has already left.`,wrongReasons:{"the train has already leave the station":`'Leave' is the base form. After 'has already' you need the past participle 'left'.`,"the train has already leaved the station":`'Leave' is irregular: leave - left - left. 'Leaved' does not exist.`},wrong:`The train has already leave the station.`,solutions:[`The train has already left the station.`]},{id:`b1-ec-007`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The present perfect uses the past participle: I have seen it twice.`,wrongReasons:{"i have saw that film twice so far":`'Saw' is the past simple. After 'have' use the past participle 'seen'.`,"i saw that film twice so far":`'So far' connects the past to now, so you need the present perfect, not the past simple.`},wrong:`I have saw that film twice so far.`,solutions:[`I have seen that film twice so far.`,`I've seen that film twice so far.`]},{id:`b1-ec-008`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'yet' at the end of negative sentences and 'already' in positive ones: I haven't eaten yet.`,wrongReasons:{"he hasn't finished his report already":`'Already' is used in positive sentences. In a negative sentence, put 'yet' at the end.`,"he hasn't finished his report still":`'Still' goes before 'hasn't' (He still hasn't...). At the end of a negative sentence use 'yet'.`},wrong:`He hasn't finished his report already.`,solutions:[`He hasn't finished his report yet.`,`He still hasn't finished his report.`,`He has already finished his report.`,`He has not finished his report yet.`,`He still has not finished his report.`,`He's already finished his report.`]},{id:`b1-ec-009`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'how long' for a situation that started in the past and continues now: How long have you known her?`,wrongReasons:{"how long are you knowing your best friend":`'How long' + a situation up to now needs the present perfect, and 'know' is not used in the continuous form.`,"how long have you been knowing your best friend":`'Know' is a state verb, so it is not used in continuous forms. Say 'have known'.`},wrong:`How long are you knowing your best friend?`,solutions:[`How long have you known your best friend?`]},{id:`b1-ec-010`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`With 'for' + a period up to now, use the present perfect: They have been married for ten years.`,wrongReasons:{"my parents are married for twenty years":`'For twenty years' up to now needs the present perfect 'have been married', not the present simple.`,"my parents have married for twenty years":`'Have married' talks about the wedding moment. For the state up to now, use 'have been married'.`},wrong:`My parents are married for twenty years.`,solutions:[`My parents have been married for twenty years.`]},{id:`b1-ec-011`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`With a finished time like 'last year' or 'yesterday', use the past simple: I saw him yesterday.`,wrongReasons:{"she has visited japan last year":`'Last year' is a finished time, so you need the past simple 'visited', not the present perfect.`,"she has visit japan last year":`This is still the present perfect (and 'visit' is not a participle). With 'last year', use the past simple 'visited'.`},wrong:`She has visited Japan last year.`,solutions:[`She visited Japan last year.`]},{id:`b1-ec-012`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'This is the first time', use the present perfect: This is the first time I have flown.`,wrongReasons:{"this is the first time i visit this museum":`After 'This is the first time' you need the present perfect, not the present simple.`,"this is the first time i am visiting this museum":`The present continuous is not used after 'This is the first time'. Use 'I have visited'.`},wrong:`This is the first time I visit this museum.`,solutions:[`This is the first time I have visited this museum.`,`This is the first time I've visited this museum.`]},{id:`b1-ec-013`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Put 'just' between have/has and the past participle: She has just called.`,wrongReasons:{"they have just arrive at the airport":`'Arrive' is the base form. After 'have just' use the past participle 'arrived'.`,"they just have arrived at the airport":`The word order is wrong: 'just' goes between 'have' and the participle.`},wrong:`They have just arrive at the airport.`,solutions:[`They have just arrived at the airport.`,`They just arrived at the airport.`,`They've just arrived at the airport.`]},{id:`b1-ec-014`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the longer action and the past simple for the short action that interrupts it: I was reading when he called.`,wrongReasons:{"i was cooking dinner when the phone was ringing":`The phone ringing is the short action that interrupts, so use the past simple 'rang'.`,"i cooked dinner when the phone was ringing":`The tenses are the wrong way round: the long action is 'was cooking', the interruption is 'rang'.`},wrong:`I was cooking dinner when the phone was ringing.`,solutions:[`I was cooking dinner when the phone rang.`]},{id:`b1-ec-015`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The past continuous is was/were + -ing: At eight we were watching TV.`,wrongReasons:{"at nine o'clock last night we were have dinner":`After 'were' you need the -ing form 'having', not the base form.`,"at nine o'clock last night we was having dinner":`'We' takes 'were', not 'was'.`},wrong:`At nine o'clock last night we were have dinner.`,solutions:[`At nine o'clock last night we were having dinner.`]},{id:`b1-ec-016`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'were' with you, we and they in the past continuous: They were running.`,wrongReasons:{"they was playing football when the storm began":`'They' needs 'were', not 'was'.`,"they were play football when the storm began":`The past continuous needs the -ing form: 'were playing'.`},wrong:`They was playing football when the storm began.`,solutions:[`They were playing football when the storm began.`]},{id:`b1-ec-017`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The past continuous is was/were + verb-ing: The kids were singing.`,wrongReasons:{"when i arrived, the guests were dance in the garden":`After 'were' you need 'dancing', not the base form 'dance'.`,"when i arrived, the guests was dancing in the garden":`'The guests' is plural, so it takes 'were', not 'was'.`},wrong:`When I arrived, the guests were dance in the garden.`,solutions:[`When I arrived, the guests were dancing in the garden.`,`When I arrived the guests were dancing in the garden.`]},{id:`b1-ec-018`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`A short, sudden action uses the past simple; the background action uses the past continuous: She fell while she was running.`,wrongReasons:{"he was breaking his arm while he was skiing":`Breaking an arm is a short, sudden action, so use the past simple 'broke'.`,"he broke his arm while he skiing":`'Was' is missing: the past continuous is 'he was skiing'.`},wrong:`He was breaking his arm while he was skiing.`,solutions:[`He broke his arm while he was skiing.`]},{id:`b1-ec-019`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Past continuous questions are was/were + subject + -ing: Were you working?`,wrongReasons:{"were you sleep when i called you":`After 'Were you' you need the -ing form 'sleeping'.`,"did you sleeping when i called you":`Past continuous questions use 'were', not 'did': 'Were you sleeping?'`},wrong:`Were you sleep when I called you?`,solutions:[`Were you sleeping when I called you?`]},{id:`b1-ec-020`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Plural subjects take 'were' in the past continuous: The students weren't talking.`,wrongReasons:{"the children wasn't listening while the teacher explained the rules":`'The children' is plural, so the negative is 'weren't', not 'wasn't'.`,"the children didn't listening while the teacher explained the rules":`The past continuous negative is 'weren't' + -ing, not 'didn't' + -ing.`},wrong:`The children wasn't listening while the teacher explained the rules.`,solutions:[`The children weren't listening while the teacher explained the rules.`,`The children weren't listening while the teacher was explaining the rules.`,`The children were not listening while the teacher explained the rules.`,`The children were not listening while the teacher was explaining the rules.`]},{id:`b1-ec-021`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`State verbs like know, like and want are not used in continuous forms: I knew her name.`,wrongReasons:{"i was knowing the answer, but i didn't say anything":`'Know' is a state verb, so use the past simple 'knew', not 'was knowing'.`,"i was know the answer, but i didn't say anything":`'Was know' is not a correct form. 'Know' is a state verb: just say 'knew'.`},wrong:`I was knowing the answer, but I didn't say anything.`,solutions:[`I knew the answer, but I didn't say anything.`,`I knew the answer, but I did not say anything.`,`I knew the answer but I did not say anything.`,`I knew the answer but I didn't say anything.`]},{id:`b1-ec-022`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The sudden action that interrupts uses the past simple: I was walking when it started to rain.`,wrongReasons:{"sara was reading a book when suddenly the lights were going out":`The lights going out is a sudden, short action, so use the past simple 'went out'.`,"sara read a book when suddenly the lights went out":`Reading is the background action in progress, so it needs the past continuous 'was reading'.`},wrong:`Sara was reading a book when suddenly the lights were going out.`,solutions:[`Sara was reading a book when suddenly the lights went out.`]},{id:`b1-ec-023`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for the event that interrupts an action in progress: What were you doing when the alarm rang?`,wrongReasons:{"what were you doing when the earthquake was happening":`The earthquake is the event that interrupts, so use the past simple 'happened'.`,"what did you do when the earthquake was happening":`The earthquake is the short event here, so it needs the past simple 'happened'.`},wrong:`What were you doing when the earthquake was happening?`,solutions:[`What were you doing when the earthquake happened?`,`What did you do when the earthquake happened?`]},{id:`b1-ec-024`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`A single, complete decision uses the past simple: It was late, so we decided to leave.`,wrongReasons:{"it was snowing heavily, so we were deciding to stay at home":`Deciding is one quick, complete action, so use the past simple 'decided'.`,"it was snowing heavily, so we were decide to stay at home":`'Were decide' is not a correct form. Use the past simple 'decided'.`},wrong:`It was snowing heavily, so we were deciding to stay at home.`,solutions:[`It was snowing heavily, so we decided to stay at home.`,`It snowed heavily, so we decided to stay at home.`,`It was snowing heavily so we decided to stay at home.`,`It snowed heavily so we decided to stay at home.`]},{id:`b1-ec-025`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In the first conditional, use the present simple after 'if' and will in the other part: If it rains, we will stay in.`,wrongReasons:{"if it will rain tomorrow, we will stay inside":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'. Use the present simple 'rains'.`,"if it rain tomorrow, we will stay inside":`'It' needs the -s form in the present simple: 'it rains'.`},wrong:`If it will rain tomorrow, we will stay inside.`,solutions:[`If it rains tomorrow, we will stay inside.`,`If it rains tomorrow we will stay inside.`,`If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay inside.`,`If it rains tomorrow we'll stay inside.`]},{id:`b1-ec-026`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The zero conditional uses the present simple in both parts for facts: If you heat water, it boils.`,wrongReasons:{"if you heat ice, it melt":`'It' needs the -s form: 'it melts'.`,"if you will heat ice, it melts":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'. Say 'If you heat ice'.`},wrong:`If you heat ice, it melt.`,solutions:[`If you heat ice, it melts.`,`If you heat ice, it will melt.`,`If you heat ice it melts.`,`If you heat ice it will melt.`,`If you heat ice, it'll melt.`,`If you heat ice it'll melt.`]},{id:`b1-ec-027`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not': Unless you study, you will fail. = If you don't study, you will fail.`,wrongReasons:{"unless you hurry, you won't miss the bus":`'Unless' means 'if not', so the sentence says the opposite of what you mean. The result should be 'you will miss the bus'.`,"unless you don't hurry, you will miss the bus":`'Unless' already means 'if not', so do not add 'don't' after it.`},wrong:`Unless you hurry, you won't miss the bus.`,solutions:[`Unless you hurry, you will miss the bus.`,`If you don't hurry, you will miss the bus.`,`If you hurry, you won't miss the bus.`,`Unless you hurry you will miss the bus.`,`Unless you hurry, you'll miss the bus.`,`Unless you hurry you'll miss the bus.`,`If you do not hurry, you will miss the bus.`,`If you do not hurry you will miss the bus.`,`If you don't hurry you will miss the bus.`,`If you do not hurry, you'll miss the bus.`,`If you do not hurry you'll miss the bus.`,`If you don't hurry, you'll miss the bus.`,`If you don't hurry you'll miss the bus.`,`If you hurry, you will not miss the bus.`,`If you hurry you will not miss the bus.`,`If you hurry you won't miss the bus.`]},{id:`b1-ec-028`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'when', 'as soon as' and 'if' about the future, use the present simple: I'll text you when I arrive.`,wrongReasons:{"i'll call you when i will get home":`Do not use 'will' after 'when' for the future. Use the present simple 'get'.`,"i'll call you when i got home":`'Got' is past. For a future time after 'when', use the present simple 'get'.`},wrong:`I'll call you when I will get home.`,solutions:[`I'll call you when I get home.`,`I will call you when I get home.`]},{id:`b1-ec-029`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For general facts, use the zero conditional with the present simple: If you mix red and white, you get pink.`,wrongReasons:{"if you mix blue and yellow, you are getting green":`For a general fact, use the present simple 'you get', not the present continuous.`,"if you mix blue and yellow, you got green":`'Got' is past. For a general fact, use the present simple 'get'.`},wrong:`If you mix blue and yellow, you are getting green.`,solutions:[`If you mix blue and yellow, you get green.`,`If you mix blue and yellow, you will get green.`,`If you mix blue and yellow you get green.`,`If you mix blue and yellow you will get green.`,`If you mix blue and yellow, you'll get green.`,`If you mix blue and yellow you'll get green.`]},{id:`b1-ec-030`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Plural subjects take the base form in the present simple: If it is too hot, the flowers die.`,wrongReasons:{"if you don't water the plants, they dies":`'They' is plural, so the verb is 'die', not 'dies'.`,"if you don't water the plants, they dying":`'Dying' needs an auxiliary, and a fact needs the present simple: 'they die'.`},wrong:`If you don't water the plants, they dies.`,solutions:[`If you don't water the plants, they die.`,`If you don't water the plants, they will die.`,`If you do not water the plants, they die.`,`If you do not water the plants they die.`,`If you don't water the plants they die.`,`If you do not water the plants, they will die.`,`If you do not water the plants they will die.`,`If you don't water the plants they will die.`,`If you do not water the plants, they'll die.`,`If you do not water the plants they'll die.`,`If you don't water the plants, they'll die.`,`If you don't water the plants they'll die.`]},{id:`b1-ec-031`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`With he, she and it, the present simple negative is 'doesn't': If she doesn't hurry, she will be late.`,wrongReasons:{"if he don't study, he will fail the exam":`'He' needs 'doesn't', not 'don't'.`,"if he not study, he will fail the exam":`The negative needs the auxiliary: 'If he doesn't study'.`},wrong:`If he don't study, he will fail the exam.`,solutions:[`If he doesn't study, he will fail the exam.`,`Unless he studies, he will fail the exam.`,`If he does not study, he will fail the exam.`,`If he does not study he will fail the exam.`,`If he doesn't study he will fail the exam.`,`If he does not study, he'll fail the exam.`,`If he does not study he'll fail the exam.`,`If he doesn't study, he'll fail the exam.`,`If he doesn't study he'll fail the exam.`,`Unless he studies he will fail the exam.`,`Unless he studies, he'll fail the exam.`,`Unless he studies he'll fail the exam.`]},{id:`b1-ec-032`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In the zero conditional, both verbs are in the present simple: If you press here, the door opens.`,wrongReasons:{"if you press this button, the machine start":`'The machine' is singular, so the verb needs -s: 'starts'.`,"if you will press this button, the machine starts":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'. Say 'If you press'.`},wrong:`If you press this button, the machine start.`,solutions:[`If you press this button, the machine starts.`,`If you press this button, the machine will start.`,`If you press this button the machine starts.`,`If you press this button the machine will start.`]},{id:`b1-ec-033`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In the first conditional, the 'if' part uses the present simple, also in the negative: If I don't pass, I will try again.`,wrongReasons:{"what will you do if you won't find a job":`After 'if' do not use 'won't'. Use the present simple negative 'don't find'.`,"what will you do if you not find a job":`The negative needs 'don't': 'if you don't find a job'.`},wrong:`What will you do if you won't find a job?`,solutions:[`What will you do if you don't find a job?`,`What will you do if you do not find a job?`]},{id:`b1-ec-034`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'if', use the present simple, not will: Tell me if you need anything.`,wrongReasons:{"call me if you will need any help":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'. Use the present simple 'need'.`,"call me if you needing any help":`'Needing' is not a complete verb. Use the present simple 'need'.`},wrong:`Call me if you will need any help.`,solutions:[`Call me if you need any help.`]},{id:`b1-ec-035`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Plural subjects use the base form in the present simple: If children sleep badly, they get tired.`,wrongReasons:{"if people eat too much sugar, they gets ill":`'They' is plural, so the verb is 'get', not 'gets'.`,"if people eats too much sugar, they get ill":`'People' is plural, so the verb is 'eat', not 'eats'.`},wrong:`If people eat too much sugar, they gets ill.`,solutions:[`If people eat too much sugar, they get ill.`,`If people eat too much sugar, they will get ill.`,`If people eat too much sugar they get ill.`,`If people eat too much sugar they will get ill.`,`If people eat too much sugar, they'll get ill.`,`If people eat too much sugar they'll get ill.`]},{id:`b1-ec-036`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The past passive is was/were + past participle: The castle was built in 1200.`,wrongReasons:{"this bridge was build in 1890":`'Build' is the base form. The passive needs the past participle 'built'.`,"this bridge was builded in 1890":`'Build' is irregular: build - built - built. 'Builded' does not exist.`},wrong:`This bridge was build in 1890.`,solutions:[`This bridge was built in 1890.`]},{id:`b1-ec-037`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The present passive is am/is/are + past participle: Spanish is spoken in Mexico.`,wrongReasons:{"english is speak in many countries":`After 'is' the passive needs the past participle 'spoken', not the base form.`,"english is speaking in many countries":`'Is speaking' is active (English is doing the action). The passive needs 'is spoken'.`},wrong:`English is speak in many countries.`,solutions:[`English is spoken in many countries.`]},{id:`b1-ec-038`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The present passive is is/are + past participle: The rooms are cleaned every day.`,wrongReasons:{"the letters are deliver every morning":`After 'are' the passive needs the past participle 'delivered'.`,"the letters are delivering every morning":`'Are delivering' is active, so the letters would do the delivering. Use 'are delivered'.`},wrong:`The letters are deliver every morning.`,solutions:[`The letters are delivered every morning.`]},{id:`b1-ec-039`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The past passive uses the past participle: My wallet was stolen.`,wrongReasons:{"my bike was stole last night":`'Stole' is the past simple. The passive needs the past participle 'stolen'.`,"my bike was stealed last night":`'Steal' is irregular: steal - stole - stolen. 'Stealed' does not exist.`},wrong:`My bike was stole last night.`,solutions:[`My bike was stolen last night.`]},{id:`b1-ec-040`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'were' with plural subjects in the past passive: The tickets were sold quickly.`,wrongReasons:{"the windows was cleaned yesterday":`'The windows' is plural, so the auxiliary is 'were', not 'was'.`,"the windows is cleaned yesterday":`'Yesterday' is past and 'windows' is plural, so use 'were cleaned'.`},wrong:`The windows was cleaned yesterday.`,solutions:[`The windows were cleaned yesterday.`]},{id:`b1-ec-041`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`When we say who did the action with 'by', use the passive: The school was opened by the queen.`,wrongReasons:{"the new hospital opened by the mayor last week":`The hospital did not open itself here: with 'by the mayor' you need the passive 'was opened'.`,"the new hospital has opened by the mayor last week":`'Has opened' is active, and 'last week' needs the past simple. Use 'was opened'.`},wrong:`The new hospital opened by the mayor last week.`,solutions:[`The new hospital was opened by the mayor last week.`]},{id:`b1-ec-042`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The passive uses the past participle (-ed for regular verbs): The flight was delayed.`,wrongReasons:{"the meeting was cancel because of the snow":`After 'was' the passive needs the past participle 'cancelled'.`,"the meeting cancelled because of the snow":`Someone cancelled the meeting, so you need the passive with 'was': 'was cancelled'.`},wrong:`The meeting was cancel because of the snow.`,solutions:[`The meeting was cancelled because of the snow.`,`The meeting was canceled because of the snow.`]},{id:`b1-ec-043`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The present passive is is/are + past participle: The park is used by many families.`,wrongReasons:{"the museum is visit by thousands of tourists every year":`After 'is' you need the past participle 'visited'.`,"the museum visits by thousands of tourists every year":`Without 'is', the museum is doing the visiting. Use the passive 'is visited'.`},wrong:`The museum is visit by thousands of tourists every year.`,solutions:[`The museum is visited by thousands of tourists every year.`]},{id:`b1-ec-044`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In the passive, use 'by' to say who did the action: The book was written by my aunt.`,wrongReasons:{"the song was written from a young singer":`To say who did the action in a passive sentence, use 'by', not 'from'.`,"the song was written of a young singer":`'Of' is not used for the person who does the action. Use 'by'.`},wrong:`The song was written from a young singer.`,solutions:[`The song was written by a young singer.`]},{id:`b1-ec-045`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Passive questions are was/were + subject + past participle: Was the parcel delivered?`,wrongReasons:{"was the email send to all the staff":`After 'Was the email' you need the past participle 'sent', not 'send'.`,"did the email sent to all the staff":`Passive questions use 'was', not 'did': 'Was the email sent?'`},wrong:`Was the email send to all the staff?`,solutions:[`Was the email sent to all the staff?`]},{id:`b1-ec-046`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The passive uses the past participle: The pizza was eaten in five minutes.`,wrongReasons:{"the cake was eat by the children":`'Eat' is the base form. The passive needs the past participle 'eaten'.`,"the cake was ate by the children":`'Ate' is the past simple. The participle is 'eaten'.`},wrong:`The cake was eat by the children.`,solutions:[`The cake was eaten by the children.`]},{id:`b1-ec-047`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Modal verbs like must, can and should are followed by the base form without 'to': You must stop here.`,wrongReasons:{"you must to wear a helmet on the building site":`Do not use 'to' after 'must'. Say 'must wear'.`,"you have wear a helmet on the building site":`'Have' needs 'to' when it means obligation: 'have to wear'.`},wrong:`You must to wear a helmet on the building site.`,solutions:[`You must wear a helmet on the building site.`,`You have to wear a helmet on the building site.`]},{id:`b1-ec-048`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After a modal verb, use the base form with no -s: He can swim.`,wrongReasons:{"she can speaks four languages":`After 'can' the verb stays in the base form: 'speak', not 'speaks'.`,"she cans speak four languages":`Modal verbs never take -s: 'she can', not 'she cans'.`},wrong:`She can speaks four languages.`,solutions:[`She can speak four languages.`]},{id:`b1-ec-049`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Mustn't' means it is not allowed; 'don't have to' means it is not necessary: You mustn't park here.`,wrongReasons:{"you don't have to smoke in the hospital":`'Don't have to' means it is not necessary. Smoking in a hospital is not allowed, so use 'mustn't'.`,"you haven't to smoke in the hospital":`'Haven't to' is not correct English. For something not allowed, say 'mustn't'.`},wrong:`You don't have to smoke in the hospital.`,solutions:[`You mustn't smoke in the hospital.`,`You can't smoke in the hospital.`,`You must not smoke in the hospital.`,`You cannot smoke in the hospital.`]},{id:`b1-ec-050`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Should' is followed by the base form without 'to': You should rest.`,wrongReasons:{"we should to book a table before we go":`Do not use 'to' after 'should'. Say 'should book'.`,"we should booking a table before we go":`After 'should' use the base form 'book', not the -ing form.`},wrong:`We should to book a table before we go.`,solutions:[`We should book a table before we go.`]},{id:`b1-ec-051`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Questions with 'must' do not use 'do'; with 'have to' they do: Do I have to pay? / Must I pay?`,wrongReasons:{"do i must bring my passport":`'Must' does not use 'do' in questions. Say 'Do I have to...?' or 'Must I...?'.`,"must i to bring my passport":`Do not use 'to' after 'must'. Say 'Must I bring...?'.`},wrong:`Do I must bring my passport?`,solutions:[`Do I have to bring my passport?`,`Must I bring my passport?`,`Do I need to bring my passport?`]},{id:`b1-ec-052`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'might' use the base form: It might rain.`,wrongReasons:{"he might comes to the party later":`After 'might' the verb stays in the base form: 'come', not 'comes'.`,"he mights come to the party later":`Modal verbs never take -s: 'he might', not 'he mights'.`},wrong:`He might comes to the party later.`,solutions:[`He might come to the party later.`]},{id:`b1-ec-053`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'could' for ability in the past: When I was ten, I could ride a bike.`,wrongReasons:{"when i was five, i can swim":`'When I was five' is in the past, so use 'could', not 'can'.`,"when i was five, i can swimming":`The time is past, so use 'could', and after a modal use the base form 'swim'.`},wrong:`When I was five, I can swim.`,solutions:[`When I was five, I could swim.`,`When I was five I could swim.`]},{id:`b1-ec-054`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Shouldn't' is followed by the base form without 'to': You shouldn't worry.`,wrongReasons:{"you shouldn't to eat so much fast food":`Do not use 'to' after 'shouldn't'. Say 'shouldn't eat'.`,"you don't should eat so much fast food":`Modal verbs do not use 'don't'. The negative is 'shouldn't'.`},wrong:`You shouldn't to eat so much fast food.`,solutions:[`You shouldn't eat so much fast food.`,`You should not eat so much fast food.`]},{id:`b1-ec-055`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Must' has no past form. For obligation in the past, use 'had to': Last week I had to work on Saturday.`,wrongReasons:{"yesterday i must work until nine o'clock":`'Must' cannot talk about the past. With 'yesterday', use 'had to'.`,"yesterday i have to work until nine o'clock":`'Have to' is present. For yesterday, use the past form 'had to'.`},wrong:`Yesterday I must work until nine o'clock.`,solutions:[`Yesterday I had to work until nine o'clock.`]},{id:`b1-ec-056`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In requests with 'could', use the base form without 'to': Could you help me?`,wrongReasons:{"could you to open the window, please":`Do not use 'to' after 'could'. Say 'Could you open...?'.`,"could you opening the window, please":`After 'could' use the base form 'open', not the -ing form.`},wrong:`Could you to open the window, please?`,solutions:[`Could you open the window, please?`,`Could you open the window please?`]},{id:`b1-ec-057`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'had better' ('d better) + base form for strong advice: You'd better leave now.`,wrongReasons:{"it's cold, so you would better take a coat":`The expression is 'had better' (short form 'you'd better'), not 'would better'.`,"it's cold, so you had better to take a coat":`Do not use 'to' after 'had better'. Say 'had better take'.`},wrong:`It's cold, so you would better take a coat.`,solutions:[`It's cold, so you had better take a coat.`,`It is cold, so you had better take a coat.`,`It is cold, so you should take a coat.`,`It's cold so you had better take a coat.`,`It's cold, so you'd better take a coat.`,`It's cold so you'd better take a coat.`,`It is cold so you had better take a coat.`,`It is cold, so you'd better take a coat.`,`It is cold so you'd better take a coat.`,`It is cold so you should take a coat.`,`It's cold, so you should take a coat.`,`It's cold so you should take a coat.`]},{id:`b1-ec-058`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base form for past habits: I used to walk to school.`,wrongReasons:{"i use to play the piano when i was a child":`For a past habit in a positive sentence, the form is 'used to', not 'use to'.`,"i was used to play the piano when i was a child":`'Be used to' means 'be familiar with' and takes -ing. For a past habit, say 'used to play'.`},wrong:`I use to play the piano when I was a child.`,solutions:[`I used to play the piano when I was a child.`]},{id:`b1-ec-059`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In questions with 'did', use 'use to' (no -d): Did you use to play tennis?`,wrongReasons:{"did you used to live in madrid":`After 'did', the verb is in the base form: 'use to', not 'used to'.`,"do you used to live in madrid":`'Used to' is about the past, so the question needs 'did', not 'do', and then 'use to'.`},wrong:`Did you used to live in Madrid?`,solutions:[`Did you use to live in Madrid?`]},{id:`b1-ec-060`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In negatives with 'didn't', use 'use to': I didn't use to drink coffee.`,wrongReasons:{"she didn't used to like vegetables":`After 'didn't', the verb is in the base form: 'use to', not 'used to'.`,"she not used to like vegetables":`The negative needs 'didn't': 'She didn't use to like...'.`},wrong:`She didn't used to like vegetables.`,solutions:[`She didn't use to like vegetables.`,`She never used to like vegetables.`,`She did not use to like vegetables.`]},{id:`b1-ec-061`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Used to' is followed by the base form of the verb: She used to live in Paris.`,wrongReasons:{"my grandfather used to working on a farm":`After 'used to' (past habit) use the base form 'work', not 'working'.`,"my grandfather use to work on a farm":`In a positive sentence about the past, the form is 'used to', with -d.`},wrong:`My grandfather used to working on a farm.`,solutions:[`My grandfather used to work on a farm.`]},{id:`b1-ec-062`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'there used to be' for things that existed in the past but not now: There used to be a shop here.`,wrongReasons:{"there use to be a cinema in our street":`In a positive sentence, the form is 'used to', not 'use to'.`,"there used to was a cinema in our street":`After 'used to' use the base form 'be', not 'was'.`},wrong:`There use to be a cinema in our street.`,solutions:[`There used to be a cinema in our street.`]},{id:`b1-ec-063`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Used to' is only for the past. For present habits use the present simple, often with 'usually': I usually walk to work.`,wrongReasons:{"i used to get up early now":`'Used to' talks about the past, but 'now' is present. Use 'usually' + present simple.`,"i use to get up early now":`'Use to' is not a way to talk about present habits. Say 'I usually get up'.`},wrong:`I used to get up early now.`,solutions:[`I usually get up early now.`,`I get up early now.`]},{id:`b1-ec-064`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In questions with 'did', use 'use to': What did you use to do at weekends?`,wrongReasons:{"where did you used to go to school":`After 'did', use the base form 'use to', not 'used to'.`,"where you used to go to school":`A question needs the auxiliary 'did': 'Where did you use to go...?'.`},wrong:`Where did you used to go to school?`,solutions:[`Where did you use to go to school?`,`Where did you go to school?`]},{id:`b1-ec-065`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Used to' is always followed by the base form: I used to have a dog.`,wrongReasons:{"when i was young, i used to had long hair":`After 'used to' use the base form 'have', not the past form 'had'.`,"when i was young, i was used to have long hair":`'Was used to' means 'was familiar with'. For a past state, say 'used to have'.`},wrong:`When I was young, I used to had long hair.`,solutions:[`When I was young, I used to have long hair.`,`When I was young, I had long hair.`,`When I was young I used to have long hair.`,`When I was young I had long hair.`]},{id:`b1-ec-066`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'used to' put the base form of the verb: We used to visit the lake.`,wrongReasons:{"they used to visited their aunt on sundays":`After 'used to' use the base form 'visit', not 'visited'.`,"they use to visit their aunt on sundays":`For a past habit in a positive sentence, write 'used to', with -d.`},wrong:`They used to visited their aunt on Sundays.`,solutions:[`They used to visit their aunt on Sundays.`]},{id:`b1-ec-067`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form describes past habits: People used to send postcards.`,wrongReasons:{"people used to writing letters before email existed":`After 'used to' (past habit) use the base form 'write', not 'writing'.`,"people were used to write letters before email existed":`'Were used to' means 'were familiar with'. For a past habit, say 'used to write'.`},wrong:`People used to writing letters before email existed.`,solutions:[`People used to write letters before email existed.`]},{id:`b1-ec-068`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Use to' in questions is followed by the base form: Did she use to sing?`,wrongReasons:{"did your dad use to working abroad":`After 'use to' use the base form 'work', not 'working'.`,"did your dad used to work abroad":`After 'did', write 'use to' without -d.`},wrong:`Did your dad use to working abroad?`,solutions:[`Did your dad use to work abroad?`]},{id:`b1-ec-069`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Some verbs like enjoy, finish and mind are followed by the -ing form: I enjoy reading.`,wrongReasons:{"i enjoy to swim in the sea":`'Enjoy' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb. Say 'enjoy swimming'.`,"i enjoy swim in the sea":`After 'enjoy' you need the -ing form 'swimming', not the base form.`},wrong:`I enjoy to swim in the sea.`,solutions:[`I enjoy swimming in the sea.`]},{id:`b1-ec-070`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Some verbs like decide, hope and promise are followed by 'to' + base form: He decided to stay.`,wrongReasons:{"she decided going to university abroad":`'Decide' is followed by 'to' + base form, not the -ing form. Say 'decided to go'.`,"she decided go to university abroad":`'To' is missing: after 'decide' use 'to go'.`},wrong:`She decided going to university abroad.`,solutions:[`She decided to go to university abroad.`]},{id:`b1-ec-071`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After a preposition (in, at, of, about) use the -ing form: She is good at drawing.`,wrongReasons:{"he is interested in learn chinese":`'In' is a preposition, so the verb after it needs -ing: 'learning'.`,"he is interested in to learn chinese":`Do not use 'to' + verb after a preposition. Use the -ing form: 'in learning'.`},wrong:`He is interested in learn Chinese.`,solutions:[`He is interested in learning Chinese.`,`He's interested in learning Chinese.`]},{id:`b1-ec-072`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Hope' is followed by 'to' + base form: I hope to win.`,wrongReasons:{"we hope seeing you again soon":`After 'hope' use 'to' + base form, not the -ing form. Say 'hope to see'.`,"we hope see you again soon":`'To' is missing after 'hope': 'hope to see'.`},wrong:`We hope seeing you again soon.`,solutions:[`We hope to see you again soon.`]},{id:`b1-ec-073`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Mind' is followed by the -ing form: Do you mind waiting?`,wrongReasons:{"do you mind to close the door":`After 'mind' use the -ing form, not 'to' + verb. Say 'mind closing'.`,"do you mind close the door":`After 'mind' you need the -ing form 'closing', not the base form.`},wrong:`Do you mind to close the door?`,solutions:[`Do you mind closing the door?`]},{id:`b1-ec-074`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by the -ing form: I look forward to hearing from you.`,wrongReasons:{"i'm looking forward to meet your family":`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so you need the -ing form 'meeting'.`,"i'm looking forward meeting your family":`'To' is missing: the expression is 'look forward to' + -ing.`},wrong:`I'm looking forward to meet your family.`,solutions:[`I'm looking forward to meeting your family.`,`I am looking forward to meeting your family.`]},{id:`b1-ec-075`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Promise' is followed by 'to' + base form: She promised to call.`,wrongReasons:{"they promised helping us with the move":`After 'promise' use 'to' + base form, not the -ing form. Say 'promised to help'.`,"they promised help us with the move":`'To' is missing after 'promised': 'promised to help'.`},wrong:`They promised helping us with the move.`,solutions:[`They promised to help us with the move.`]},{id:`b1-ec-076`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`When a verb is the subject of a sentence, use the -ing form: Walking is good exercise.`,wrongReasons:{"swim every day is good for your heart":`A verb used as the subject needs the -ing form: 'Swimming every day is...'.`,"swam every day is good for your heart":`'Swam' is the past simple. As the subject of the sentence, use 'Swimming'.`},wrong:`Swim every day is good for your heart.`,solutions:[`Swimming every day is good for your heart.`,`To swim every day is good for your heart.`]},{id:`b1-ec-077`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Avoid' is followed by the -ing form: He avoided looking at me.`,wrongReasons:{"she avoided to answer the question":`After 'avoid' use the -ing form, not 'to' + verb. Say 'avoided answering'.`,"she avoided answer the question":`After 'avoid' you need the -ing form 'answering', not the base form.`},wrong:`She avoided to answer the question.`,solutions:[`She avoided answering the question.`]},{id:`b1-ec-078`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Afford' is followed by 'to' + base form: We can't afford to travel this year.`,wrongReasons:{"i can't afford buying a new car":`After 'afford' use 'to' + base form, not the -ing form. Say 'afford to buy'.`,"i can't afford buy a new car":`'To' is missing after 'afford': 'afford to buy'.`},wrong:`I can't afford buying a new car.`,solutions:[`I can't afford to buy a new car.`,`I cannot afford to buy a new car.`]},{id:`b1-ec-079`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After phrasal verbs like give up and keep on, use the -ing form: She gave up smoking.`,wrongReasons:{"he gave up to eat meat last year":`After 'give up' use the -ing form, not 'to' + verb. Say 'gave up eating'.`,"he gave up eat meat last year":`After 'give up' you need the -ing form 'eating', not the base form.`},wrong:`He gave up to eat meat last year.`,solutions:[`He gave up eating meat last year.`]},{id:`b1-ec-080`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The form is be + going to + base form: It is going to snow.`,wrongReasons:{"look at those dark clouds! it is going rain":`'To' is missing: the form is 'going to rain'.`,"look at those dark clouds! it going to rain":`The verb 'be' is missing: say 'It is going to rain' or 'It's going to rain'.`},wrong:`Look at those dark clouds! It is going rain.`,solutions:[`Look at those dark clouds! It is going to rain.`,`Look at those dark clouds! It's going to rain.`]},{id:`b1-ec-081`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For fixed plans, use the present continuous (be + -ing): We are flying to Rome on Friday.`,wrongReasons:{"we are meet the clients tomorrow morning":`After 'are' you need the -ing form 'meeting' for a future plan.`,"we meeting the clients tomorrow morning":`The auxiliary 'are' is missing: 'We are meeting'.`},wrong:`We are meet the clients tomorrow morning.`,solutions:[`We are meeting the clients tomorrow morning.`,`We are going to meet the clients tomorrow morning.`,`We will meet the clients tomorrow morning.`,`We're meeting the clients tomorrow morning.`,`We're going to meet the clients tomorrow morning.`,`We'll meet the clients tomorrow morning.`]},{id:`b1-ec-082`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Will' never changes: I will, she will, they will + base form.`,wrongReasons:{"i think she wills pass the exam":`'Will' does not take -s. Say 'she will pass'.`,"i think she will passes the exam":`After 'will' use the base form 'pass', not 'passes'.`},wrong:`I think she wills pass the exam.`,solutions:[`I think she will pass the exam.`,`I think she'll pass the exam.`]},{id:`b1-ec-083`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For timetables, use the present simple; in questions with 'does', the verb is in the base form: What time does the film start?`,wrongReasons:{"what time does the concert starts tomorrow":`After 'does' use the base form 'start', not 'starts'.`,"what time the concert starts tomorrow":`A question needs the auxiliary 'does': 'What time does the concert start?'.`},wrong:`What time does the concert starts tomorrow?`,solutions:[`What time does the concert start tomorrow?`,`What time will the concert start tomorrow?`,`What time is the concert starting tomorrow?`]},{id:`b1-ec-084`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'going to' use the base form: Are you going to stay?`,wrongReasons:{"are you going to coming to the meeting":`After 'going to' use the base form 'come', not 'coming'.`,"are you going come to the meeting":`'To' is missing: the form is 'going to come'.`},wrong:`Are you going to coming to the meeting?`,solutions:[`Are you going to come to the meeting?`,`Are you coming to the meeting?`]},{id:`b1-ec-085`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for offers and decisions made at the moment of speaking: I'll open the door for you.`,wrongReasons:{"don't worry, i carry that bag for you":`For an offer you make now, use 'will' ('I'll carry'), not the present simple.`,"don't worry, i'm carrying that bag for you":`The present continuous is for plans already made. For a quick offer, use 'I'll carry'.`},wrong:`Don't worry, I carry that bag for you.`,solutions:[`Don't worry, I will carry that bag for you.`,`Don't worry, I can carry that bag for you.`,`Don't worry I will carry that bag for you.`,`Don't worry, I'll carry that bag for you.`,`Don't worry I'll carry that bag for you.`,`Don't worry I can carry that bag for you.`]},{id:`b1-ec-086`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the present simple for timetables: The bus leaves at eight.`,wrongReasons:{"the train leave at 6:45 tomorrow morning":`'The train' is singular, so the verb needs -s: 'leaves'.`,"the train leaving at 6:45 tomorrow morning":`'Leaving' alone is not a full verb. For a timetable, say 'leaves'.`},wrong:`The train leave at 6:45 tomorrow morning.`,solutions:[`The train leaves at 6:45 tomorrow morning.`,`The train will leave at 6:45 tomorrow morning.`,`The train is leaving at 6:45 tomorrow morning.`]},{id:`b1-ec-087`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Going to' is always followed by the base form: He is going to buy a car.`,wrongReasons:{"she's going to has a baby in may":`After 'going to' use the base form 'have', not 'has'.`,"she's going to having a baby in may":`After 'going to' use the base form 'have', not the -ing form.`},wrong:`She's going to has a baby in May.`,solutions:[`She's going to have a baby in May.`,`She is going to have a baby in May.`,`She is having a baby in May.`,`She's having a baby in May.`]},{id:`b1-ec-088`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Will' is followed by the base form without 'to': Will you come?`,wrongReasons:{"will you to help me tomorrow":`Do not use 'to' after 'will'. Say 'Will you help...?'.`,"will you helping me tomorrow":`After 'will' use the base form 'help', not the -ing form.`},wrong:`Will you to help me tomorrow?`,solutions:[`Will you help me tomorrow?`]},{id:`b1-ec-089`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'will' use the base form: You will love it.`,wrongReasons:{"i'm sure you will enjoying the film":`After 'will' use the base form 'enjoy', not 'enjoying'.`,"i'm sure you will enjoys the film":`After 'will' the verb has no -s: 'will enjoy'.`},wrong:`I'm sure you will enjoying the film.`,solutions:[`I'm sure you will enjoy the film.`,`I am sure you will enjoy the film.`,`I am sure you'll enjoy the film.`,`I'm sure you'll enjoy the film.`]},{id:`b1-ec-090`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For plans, use be + going to + base form: I am going to learn Spanish.`,wrongReasons:{"my brother is go to start a new job next month":`The form is 'is going to', not 'is go to'.`,"my brother go to start a new job next month":`This needs 'is going to': 'My brother is going to start...'.`},wrong:`My brother is go to start a new job next month.`,solutions:[`My brother is going to start a new job next month.`,`My brother is starting a new job next month.`,`My brother will start a new job next month.`]},{id:`b1-ec-091`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people and 'which' or 'that' for things: The man who called is my boss.`,wrongReasons:{"the woman which lives next door is a nurse":`'Which' is for things. For a person, use 'who' or 'that'.`,"the woman what lives next door is a nurse":`'What' is not used as a relative pronoun after a noun. Use 'who' or 'that'.`},wrong:`The woman which lives next door is a nurse.`,solutions:[`The woman who lives next door is a nurse.`,`The woman that lives next door is a nurse.`]},{id:`b1-ec-092`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for places, or 'which/that' + preposition: This is the house where I grew up.`,wrongReasons:{"this is the town who i was born in":`'Who' is for people. For a place, use 'where' (without 'in') or 'that/which ... in'.`,"this is the town where i was born in":`'Where' already means 'in which', so do not add 'in' at the end.`},wrong:`This is the town who I was born in.`,solutions:[`This is the town where I was born.`,`This is the town that I was born in.`,`This is the town which I was born in.`,`This is the town I was born in.`]},{id:`b1-ec-093`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'which' or 'that' for things (or nothing when it is the object): The cake that you made was great.`,wrongReasons:{"i lost the book who you gave me":`'Who' is for people. For a thing like a book, use 'that' or 'which'.`,"i lost the book what you gave me":`'What' cannot follow a noun as a relative pronoun. Use 'that' or 'which'.`},wrong:`I lost the book who you gave me.`,solutions:[`I lost the book that you gave me.`,`I lost the book which you gave me.`,`I lost the book you gave me.`]},{id:`b1-ec-094`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In a relative clause, 'who' replaces the subject, so do not add another pronoun: The girl who won is my cousin.`,wrongReasons:{"the man who he called you is my uncle":`'Who' is already the subject of 'called', so remove 'he'.`,"the man which called you is my uncle":`'Which' is for things. For a man, use 'who' or 'that'.`},wrong:`The man who he called you is my uncle.`,solutions:[`The man who called you is my uncle.`,`The man that called you is my uncle.`]},{id:`b1-ec-095`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for a place where something happens: This is the park where we met.`,wrongReasons:{"is this the restaurant which we had lunch last year":`'Which' needs a preposition here ('which ... in'). The simplest choice for a place is 'where'.`,"is this the restaurant what we had lunch last year":`'What' is not a relative pronoun after a noun. For a place, use 'where'.`},wrong:`Is this the restaurant which we had lunch last year?`,solutions:[`Is this the restaurant where we had lunch last year?`,`Is this the restaurant that we had lunch in last year?`,`Is this the restaurant which we had lunch in last year?`,`Is this the restaurant in which we had lunch last year?`]},{id:`b1-ec-096`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people: A pilot is a person who flies planes.`,wrongReasons:{"a dentist is a person which looks after your teeth":`A dentist is a person, so use 'who' or 'that', not 'which'.`,"a dentist is a person who look after your teeth":`'Who' refers to 'a person' (singular), so the verb needs -s: 'looks'.`},wrong:`A dentist is a person which looks after your teeth.`,solutions:[`A dentist is a person who looks after your teeth.`,`A dentist is a person that looks after your teeth.`]},{id:`b1-ec-097`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For things, use 'that' or 'which' (or nothing when it is the object): The bag that I bought is red.`,wrongReasons:{"the phone what i bought last week has stopped working":`'What' cannot follow a noun. Use 'that' or 'which', or leave it out.`,"the phone who i bought last week has stopped working":`'Who' is for people. A phone is a thing, so use 'that' or 'which'.`},wrong:`The phone what I bought last week has stopped working.`,solutions:[`The phone that I bought last week has stopped working.`,`The phone which I bought last week has stopped working.`,`The phone I bought last week has stopped working.`]},{id:`b1-ec-098`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'whose' to show possession in a relative clause: That's the man whose car is blue.`,wrongReasons:{"do you know the girl who her bag was stolen":`To say that the bag belongs to the girl, use 'whose bag', not 'who her bag'.`,"do you know the girl who's bag was stolen":`'Who's' means 'who is'. For possession, write 'whose'.`},wrong:`Do you know the girl who her bag was stolen?`,solutions:[`Do you know the girl whose bag was stolen?`]},{id:`b1-ec-099`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Where' already means 'in that place', so do not repeat 'there': This is the shop where I work.`,wrongReasons:{"that's the bakery where i usually buy bread there":`'Where' already refers to the bakery, so remove 'there' at the end.`,"that's the bakery which i usually buy bread there":`'Which' needs a preposition and 'there' repeats the place. Say 'where I usually buy bread'.`},wrong:`That's the bakery where I usually buy bread there.`,solutions:[`That's the bakery where I usually buy bread.`,`That is the bakery where I usually buy bread.`]},{id:`b1-ec-100`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people: The players who scored were happy.`,wrongReasons:{"the students which passed the test got a certificate":`Students are people, so use 'who' or 'that', not 'which'.`,"the students whom passed the test got a certificate":`'Whom' is only for the object. Here the students do the action, so use 'who'.`},wrong:`The students which passed the test got a certificate.`,solutions:[`The students who passed the test got a certificate.`,`The students that passed the test got a certificate.`]},{id:`b1-ec-101`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'which' or 'that' for things and places when they are the subject: We ate at a cafe that had great cakes.`,wrongReasons:{"we stayed in a hotel who had a beautiful pool":`A hotel is not a person, so use 'which' or 'that', not 'who'.`,"we stayed in a hotel where had a beautiful pool":`'Where' must be followed by a subject and verb (where we swam). Here use 'which' or 'that'.`},wrong:`We stayed in a hotel who had a beautiful pool.`,solutions:[`We stayed in a hotel which had a beautiful pool.`,`We stayed in a hotel that had a beautiful pool.`]},{id:`b1-ec-102`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Turn on' means start a machine or light; 'turn off' means stop it: Turn off the TV before bed.`,wrongReasons:{"please turn on the lights when you leave the room":`When you leave a room you stop the lights, so the verb is 'turn off', not 'turn on'.`,"please turn down the lights when you leave the room":`'Turn down' means make lower or quieter. To stop the lights completely, say 'turn off'.`},wrong:`Please turn on the lights when you leave the room.`,solutions:[`Please turn off the lights when you leave the room.`,`Please switch off the lights when you leave the room.`,`Please turn the lights off when you leave the room.`,`Please switch the lights off when you leave the room.`]},{id:`b1-ec-103`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Look for' means try to find; 'look after' means take care of: I'm looking for my glasses.`,wrongReasons:{"i'm looking after my keys. have you seen them":`'Look after' means take care of. To try to find something, use 'look for'.`,"i'm looking at my keys. have you seen them":`'Look at' means point your eyes at something. If you can't find them, say 'looking for'.`},wrong:`I'm looking after my keys. Have you seen them?`,solutions:[`I'm looking for my keys. Have you seen them?`,`I am looking for my keys. Have you seen them?`]},{id:`b1-ec-104`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`With phrasal verbs like pick up, an object pronoun goes in the middle: Can you pick her up?`,wrongReasons:{"can you pick up me at the station":`The pronoun 'me' must go between 'pick' and 'up': 'pick me up'.`,"can you pick me at the station up":`'Up' must come straight after the pronoun: 'pick me up at the station'.`},wrong:`Can you pick up me at the station?`,solutions:[`Can you pick me up at the station?`,`Could you pick me up at the station?`]},{id:`b1-ec-105`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Break down' means stop working (for cars and machines): The bus broke down.`,wrongReasons:{"my car broke up on the motorway":`'Break up' means end a relationship. A car that stops working 'breaks down'.`,"my car broke off on the motorway":`'Break off' means separate a piece from something. For a car that stops working, use 'broke down'.`},wrong:`My car broke up on the motorway.`,solutions:[`My car broke down on the motorway.`]},{id:`b1-ec-106`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Run out of' means have no more of something: We've run out of bread.`,wrongReasons:{"we ran out from milk, so i went to the shop":`The phrasal verb is 'run out of', not 'run out from'.`,"we ran off milk, so i went to the shop":`'Run off' means leave quickly. To have no more of something, say 'run out of'.`},wrong:`We ran out from milk, so I went to the shop.`,solutions:[`We ran out of milk, so I went to the shop.`,`We ran out of milk so I went to the shop.`]},{id:`b1-ec-107`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Fill in' (or 'fill out') means write information on a form: Fill in your details here.`,wrongReasons:{"please fill on this form with your name and address":`The phrasal verb for forms is 'fill in' or 'fill out', not 'fill on'.`,"please fill up this form with your name and address":`'Fill up' means make something full, like a glass or a car. For a form, say 'fill in'.`},wrong:`Please fill on this form with your name and address.`,solutions:[`Please fill in this form with your name and address.`,`Please fill out this form with your name and address.`,`Please fill this form in with your name and address.`]},{id:`b1-ec-108`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Find out' means get information about something: Can you find out the price?`,wrongReasons:{"i need to find up what time the museum opens":`To get information, the phrasal verb is 'find out', not 'find up'.`,"i need to found out what time the museum opens":`After 'need to' use the base form 'find', not 'found'.`},wrong:`I need to find up what time the museum opens.`,solutions:[`I need to find out what time the museum opens.`]},{id:`b1-ec-109`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Take after' means look or behave like an older family member: He takes after his dad.`,wrongReasons:{"anna takes over her mother. they both have curly hair":`'Take over' means take control of something. To be similar to a parent, say 'takes after'.`,"anna looks after her mother. they both have curly hair":`'Look after' means take care of someone. Being similar to a parent is 'take after'.`},wrong:`Anna takes over her mother. They both have curly hair.`,solutions:[`Anna takes after her mother. They both have curly hair.`]},{id:`b1-ec-110`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Turn up' means make louder; 'turn down' means make quieter: Please turn the TV down.`,wrongReasons:{"could you turn the music up? it's too loud":`If the music is too loud, you want it quieter, so say 'turn the music down', not 'up'.`,"could you turn the music on? it's too loud":`'Turn on' means start it. If it is already too loud, say 'turn it down'.`},wrong:`Could you turn the music up? It's too loud.`,solutions:[`Could you turn the music down? It's too loud.`,`Could you turn the music down? It is too loud.`,`Could you turn down the music? It is too loud.`,`Could you turn the music off? It is too loud.`,`Could you turn off the music? It is too loud.`,`Could you turn down the music? It's too loud.`,`Could you turn the music off? It's too loud.`,`Could you turn off the music? It's too loud.`]},{id:`b1-ec-111`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Get on (well) with someone' means have a good relationship: She gets on well with her sister.`,wrongReasons:{"i get on well to my new colleagues":`The phrasal verb is 'get on with' someone, not 'get on to'.`,"i get on well at my new colleagues":`'At' is not used here. You 'get on well with' people.`},wrong:`I get on well to my new colleagues.`,solutions:[`I get on well with my new colleagues.`]},{id:`b1-ec-112`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Put off' means move to a later time: They put off the trip until June.`,wrongReasons:{"the meeting was put out until next friday":`To move an event to a later date, use 'put off', not 'put out'.`,"the meeting was put away until next friday":`'Put away' means put something back in its place. For a later date, say 'put off'.`},wrong:`The meeting was put out until next Friday.`,solutions:[`The meeting was put off until next Friday.`,`The meeting was postponed until next Friday.`]},{id:`b1-ec-113`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'although' or 'but' to show contrast, never both in the same sentence: Although it was late, we stayed.`,wrongReasons:{"although it was raining, but we went for a walk":`Do not use 'although' and 'but' together. Keep only one of them.`,"although it was raining, however we went for a walk":`'However' cannot join with 'although' like this. Just say 'Although it was raining, we went...'.`},wrong:`Although it was raining, but we went for a walk.`,solutions:[`Although it was raining, we went for a walk.`,`It was raining, but we went for a walk.`,`Although it was raining we went for a walk.`,`It was raining but we went for a walk.`]},{id:`b1-ec-114`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'So' introduces a result; 'because' introduces a reason: It was hot, so I opened the window.`,wrongReasons:{"i was tired, because i went to bed early":`Going to bed early is the result of being tired, so use 'so', not 'because'.`,"i was tired, although i went to bed early":`'Although' shows contrast, but there is no contrast here. The second part is a result, so use 'so'.`},wrong:`I was tired, because I went to bed early.`,solutions:[`I was tired, so I went to bed early.`,`I went to bed early because I was tired.`,`I was tired so I went to bed early.`]},{id:`b1-ec-115`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Because' gives the reason: I was late because the train stopped.`,wrongReasons:{"she missed the bus so she got up late":`Getting up late is the reason, not the result, so use 'because'.`,"she missed the bus although she got up late":`'Although' shows contrast. Getting up late is the reason, so use 'because'.`},wrong:`She missed the bus so she got up late.`,solutions:[`She missed the bus because she got up late.`,`She got up late, so she missed the bus.`,`She got up late so she missed the bus.`]},{id:`b1-ec-116`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'so' for a result and 'but' for a contrast: It was raining, so we stayed in.`,wrongReasons:{"she was ill, but she didn't go to work":`Not going to work is a result of being ill, not a contrast, so use 'so'.`,"she was ill, although she didn't go to work":`'Although' shows contrast, but here we have a cause and a result. Use 'so'.`},wrong:`She was ill, but she didn't go to work.`,solutions:[`She was ill, so she didn't go to work.`,`She didn't go to work because she was ill.`,`Because she was ill, she didn't go to work.`,`She was ill, so she did not go to work.`,`She was ill so she did not go to work.`,`She was ill so she didn't go to work.`,`She did not go to work because she was ill.`,`Because she was ill, she did not go to work.`,`Because she was ill she did not go to work.`,`Because she was ill she didn't go to work.`]},{id:`b1-ec-117`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Although' is followed by a subject and verb; 'despite' is followed by a noun: Although it was late... / Despite the rain...`,wrongReasons:{"despite it was cold, he wasn't wearing a coat":`'Despite' cannot be followed by subject + verb (it was). Use 'Although' or 'Even though'.`,"despite of it was cold, he wasn't wearing a coat":`'Despite of' is never correct, and a clause after it is wrong too. Use 'Although it was cold'.`},wrong:`Despite it was cold, he wasn't wearing a coat.`,solutions:[`Although it was cold, he wasn't wearing a coat.`,`Even though it was cold, he wasn't wearing a coat.`,`Though it was cold, he wasn't wearing a coat.`,`Despite the cold, he wasn't wearing a coat.`,`Although it was cold, he was not wearing a coat.`,`Although it was cold he was not wearing a coat.`,`Although it was cold he wasn't wearing a coat.`,`Even though it was cold, he was not wearing a coat.`,`Even though it was cold he was not wearing a coat.`,`Even though it was cold he wasn't wearing a coat.`,`Though it was cold, he was not wearing a coat.`,`Though it was cold he was not wearing a coat.`,`Though it was cold he wasn't wearing a coat.`,`Despite the cold, he was not wearing a coat.`,`Despite the cold he was not wearing a coat.`,`Despite the cold he wasn't wearing a coat.`]},{id:`b1-ec-118`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'but' or 'although' for a surprising contrast: She trained a lot, but she lost.`,wrongReasons:{"he studied hard, so he failed the exam":`Failing after studying hard is a contrast, not a result, so use 'but'.`,"he studied hard, because he failed the exam":`'Because' gives a reason, but failing is not the reason for studying. Use 'but'.`},wrong:`He studied hard, so he failed the exam.`,solutions:[`He studied hard, but he failed the exam.`,`Although he studied hard, he failed the exam.`,`He didn't study hard, so he failed the exam.`,`He studied hard but he failed the exam.`,`Although he studied hard he failed the exam.`,`He did not study hard, so he failed the exam.`,`He did not study hard so he failed the exam.`,`He didn't study hard so he failed the exam.`]},{id:`b1-ec-119`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Because' is followed by subject + verb; 'because of' is followed by a noun: because it rained / because of the rain.`,wrongReasons:{"we stayed at home because of it was snowing":`After 'because of' you need a noun. Before a subject and verb (it was), use only 'because'.`,"we stayed at home so it was snowing":`'So' introduces a result, but the snow is the reason. Use 'because'.`},wrong:`We stayed at home because of it was snowing.`,solutions:[`We stayed at home because it was snowing.`,`We stayed at home because of the snow.`]},{id:`b1-ec-120`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'In case' + subject + verb means 'because it might happen': Take a map in case you get lost.`,wrongReasons:{"take an umbrella in case of it rains":`Before a subject and verb (it rains), use 'in case', not 'in case of'.`,"take an umbrella if case it rains":`'If case' is not an English expression. Say 'in case it rains'.`},wrong:`Take an umbrella in case of it rains.`,solutions:[`Take an umbrella in case it rains.`]},{id:`b1-ec-121`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'then', 'next' or 'after that' to show the next step: First, wash the rice. Then, cook it.`,wrongReasons:{"first, mix the flour and the sugar. than, add the eggs":`'Than' is used in comparisons (bigger than). For the next step, write 'Then'.`,"first, mix the flour and the sugar. after, add the eggs":`'After' alone is not used for the next step. Say 'Then' or 'After that'.`},wrong:`First, mix the flour and the sugar. Than, add the eggs.`,solutions:[`First, mix the flour and the sugar. Then, add the eggs.`,`First, mix the flour and the sugar. After that, add the eggs.`,`First, mix the flour and the sugar. Next, add the eggs.`,`First mix the flour and the sugar. Then add the eggs.`,`First mix the flour and the sugar. After that add the eggs.`,`First mix the flour and the sugar. Next add the eggs.`]},{id:`b1-ec-122`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'because of' before a noun: We were late because of the traffic.`,wrongReasons:{"i couldn't sleep because the noise":`'The noise' is a noun, so you need 'because of', not just 'because'.`,"i couldn't sleep so the noise":`'So' introduces a result and needs a verb. Before a noun, use 'because of'.`},wrong:`I couldn't sleep because the noise.`,solutions:[`I couldn't sleep because of the noise.`,`I could not sleep because of the noise.`]},{id:`b1-ec-123`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'So' introduces a result: The film was boring, so we left.`,wrongReasons:{"the shop was closed, so that we went home":`For a result, use 'so', not 'so that' ('so that' shows purpose).`,"the shop was closed, because we went home":`'Because' gives a reason, but going home is the result. Use 'so'.`},wrong:`The shop was closed, so that we went home.`,solutions:[`The shop was closed, so we went home.`,`The shop was closed so we went home.`]},{id:`b1-ec-124`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`You 'lend' something to someone and you 'borrow' something from someone: Can you lend me a pound?`,wrongReasons:{"can you borrow me your pen, please":`'Borrow' means take from someone. When the other person gives it, use 'lend'.`,"can you rent me your pen, please":`'Rent' means pay money to use something. For a pen between friends, say 'lend'.`},wrong:`Can you borrow me your pen, please?`,solutions:[`Can you lend me your pen, please?`,`Could you lend me your pen, please?`,`Can I borrow your pen, please?`,`Could I borrow your pen, please?`,`Can you lend me your pen please?`,`Could you lend me your pen please?`,`Can I borrow your pen please?`,`Could I borrow your pen please?`]},{id:`b1-ec-125`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`We say 'make a mistake', not 'do a mistake': Everyone makes mistakes.`,wrongReasons:{"i did a lot of mistakes in the test":`The correct collocation is 'make mistakes', not 'do mistakes'.`,"i took a lot of mistakes in the test":`'Take' is not used with 'mistakes'. Say 'made a lot of mistakes'.`},wrong:`I did a lot of mistakes in the test.`,solutions:[`I made a lot of mistakes in the test.`]},{id:`b1-ec-126`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Sensitive' means easily upset by feelings; 'sensible' means practical and wise: She is sensitive to criticism.`,wrongReasons:{"my little brother is very sensible, so he often cries at sad films":`'Sensible' means practical. Someone who cries easily is 'sensitive'.`,"my little brother is very sensational, so he often cries at sad films":`'Sensational' means very exciting or shocking. The right word is 'sensitive'.`},wrong:`My little brother is very sensible, so he often cries at sad films.`,solutions:[`My little brother is very sensitive, so he often cries at sad films.`,`My little brother is very sensitive so he often cries at sad films.`]},{id:`b1-ec-127`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use -ing adjectives for the thing and -ed adjectives for how people feel: The lesson was boring, so I was bored.`,wrongReasons:{"the film was so bored that i fell asleep":`The film causes the feeling, so it is 'boring'. People feel 'bored'.`,"the film was so bore that i fell asleep":`'Bore' is a verb or noun. You need the adjective 'boring'.`},wrong:`The film was so bored that I fell asleep.`,solutions:[`The film was so boring that I fell asleep.`]},{id:`b1-ec-128`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Agree' is a verb, so do not use 'am' before it: I agree with Tom.`,wrongReasons:{"i'm agree with you":`'Agree' is a verb, not an adjective, so do not use 'am'. Say 'I agree'.`,"i'm agreeing with you":`'Agree' is a state verb and is not usually used in the continuous form. Say 'I agree'.`},wrong:`I'm agree with you.`,solutions:[`I agree with you.`]},{id:`b1-ec-129`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Travel' is usually a verb or uncountable noun; for one visit, say 'a trip' or 'a journey': We had a short trip to Paris.`,wrongReasons:{"we had a great travel to portugal last summer":`You cannot say 'a travel'. For one visit to a place, use 'a trip'.`,"we had a great travels to portugal last summer":`'A' cannot go with a plural, and 'travels' is not the word here. Say 'a great trip'.`},wrong:`We had a great travel to Portugal last summer.`,solutions:[`We had a great trip to Portugal last summer.`,`We had a great journey to Portugal last summer.`]},{id:`b1-ec-130`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`We 'tell' someone something, but we 'say' something (to someone): Tell me your name.`,wrongReasons:{"can you say me the time, please":`'Say' is not followed directly by a person. Use 'tell me'.`,"can you speak me the time, please":`'Speak' is not used with a person + information. Say 'tell me the time'.`},wrong:`Can you say me the time, please?`,solutions:[`Can you tell me the time, please?`,`Could you tell me the time, please?`,`Can you tell me the time please?`,`Could you tell me the time please?`]},{id:`b1-ec-131`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'News' is uncountable and takes a singular verb: The news is on at six.`,wrongReasons:{"the news are very bad today":`'News' looks plural but is uncountable, so use 'is', not 'are'.`,"the newses are very bad today":`'News' has no plural form 'newses'. Say 'The news is'.`},wrong:`The news are very bad today.`,solutions:[`The news is very bad today.`]},{id:`b1-ec-132`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Information' is uncountable, so it has no plural: Can you give me some information?`,wrongReasons:{"i need some informations about the course":`'Information' is uncountable, so it has no -s.`,"i need an information about the course":`Uncountable nouns cannot take 'a/an'. Say 'some information'.`},wrong:`I need some informations about the course.`,solutions:[`I need some information about the course.`]},{id:`b1-ec-133`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`A 'cook' or 'chef' is a person; a 'cooker' is a machine in the kitchen: My aunt is a great cook.`,wrongReasons:{"my father is a cooker in a big hotel":`A 'cooker' is the machine you cook on. The person is a 'cook' or a 'chef'.`,"my father is a cooking in a big hotel":`'Cooking' is the activity, not a person. Say 'a cook'.`},wrong:`My father is a cooker in a big hotel.`,solutions:[`My father is a cook in a big hotel.`,`My father is a chef in a big hotel.`]},{id:`b1-ec-134`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`We say 'have a party' or 'throw a party', not 'do a party': We're having a party on Saturday.`,wrongReasons:{"let's do a party for her birthday":`'Do' is not used with 'party'. Say 'have a party'.`,"let's make a party for her birthday":`'Make' is not used with 'party' either. The natural verb is 'have' or 'throw'.`},wrong:`Let's do a party for her birthday!`,solutions:[`Let's have a party for her birthday!`,`Let's throw a party for her birthday!`,`Let's organise a party for her birthday!`,`Let's organize a party for her birthday!`]},{id:`b1-ec-135`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Remind someone to do something' means help them not forget: Remind me to call Mum.`,wrongReasons:{"remember me to buy some bread":`When another person helps you not forget, the verb is 'remind', not 'remember'.`,"remember to me to buy some bread":`'Remember to me' is not English. Say 'Remind me to buy'.`},wrong:`Remember me to buy some bread.`,solutions:[`Remind me to buy some bread.`,`Remember to buy some bread.`]}]};export{e as default};